#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639} :activity/id #uuid "39af6225-7719-5f09-ae8c-6df89ff6b7cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048280 :topic/id #uuid "900f65f4-ad2b-51fa-b027-f2b2cba3065b" :topic/math-academy-id 3754} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180661 :tutorial/content "If we start with the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} ,\n$$\n\nwe can use the relationship between multiplication and division to write down a formula to calculate a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how to apply this formula using a real-world example.\n\nSuppose that $54$ out of $300$ boxes of grapes in a supermarket contain red grapes. What percentage of the boxes contain red grapes?\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of red grapes, which is $54$, and the whole is the total number of grapes, which is $300.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 54 \\div 300 = \\dfrac{54}{300}.\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{54}{300} = \\dfrac{54 \\div 3}{300 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{18}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{18}{100} = 18 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18 \\%$ of the boxes contain red grapes." :tutorial/id #uuid "73bca8be-ae46-5214-9ba6-2beb553be49f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2082 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bdf65be2-4038-5c62-b8e0-2362d10939cc" :step/math-academy-id 7964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179496 :question/id #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" :question/math-academy-id "e-2333"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050776}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538994}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"} :step/id #uuid "2a3797e0-441d-5d87-adba-4fa2a33a82b4" :step/math-academy-id 7965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180641}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179497 :question/id #uuid "2f3270cc-d191-5282-b9f7-cf63dcd69d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-2334"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459309}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"} :step/id #uuid "d0c640ea-bd66-5449-a695-d9913915d65f" :step/math-academy-id 7966}] :activity/title "Finding a Percentage Given Two Numbers: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179897}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199284} :activity/id #uuid "ea08cd43-7cea-59a6-a6eb-b25618d785bd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048429 :topic/id #uuid "9eaae820-5193-5702-9ef4-efac567af4b5" :topic/math-academy-id 4136} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199466 :tutorial/content "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](images/68/6816c161288078cb818415f78c12133bae8aa85933eae82f74f6601b83142c35.svg)\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n5 \\div 9 = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{5}{9}}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "331c6a05-6da8-51c8-84ad-b69ff3dd9f56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7635 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77d5039f-8317-56ed-9c58-9503a2ff03cc" :step/math-academy-id 44285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198023 :question/id #uuid "41696b59-d81e-5c49-a967-141f67934aa1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12359"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a25c72-9fa7-58fc-bf45-9cc2761696ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050719}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447644}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "04ca83ac-7a80-5eb8-8fb9-98dc863ddddb" :step/math-academy-id 44286 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198024 :question/id #uuid "e40e5f9e-aab7-53ed-8850-bd855f2b97a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12360"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fe851f3-482c-5426-87b2-32ec3c1b40f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048768}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531656}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Mixed Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "68dba314-3daa-559b-9888-94f883e881ef" :step/math-academy-id 44287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198025 :question/id #uuid "a650b9e6-c97c-5471-af2d-76a5cfb0caff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12361"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d17ce34f-7d65-5f44-a1c0-dfd3c2ea55f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447643}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "af22cc6e-47b4-5a18-a2b1-50d51596813e" :step/math-academy-id 44288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199467 :tutorial/content "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-8}\n$$\n\nA negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{-41}{8} = \\dfrac{41}{-8}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-8}}).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $({{\\color{blue}41}}){:}$\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{blue}41}}{-8}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "652d8025-fc3e-56b0-ac6a-10bc44ecaf15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7636 :tutorial/title "Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "c1d49b55-ca98-5e16-a9de-14e2aaf4fe80" :step/math-academy-id 44289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199287}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198482}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298} :activity/id #uuid "b42cbc99-dc3a-5ea5-af15-2e43d6dacdce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153325 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%,$ convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160.$ \n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200.$\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." :tutorial/id #uuid "83dbd64f-3ab9-5239-b54b-ae6d91816248" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14336 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "547ba33b-b6b3-5bae-b2dc-eacefe1e81a4" :step/math-academy-id 615692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151899 :question/id #uuid "2cfd1ed7-8249-5970-9ddc-1bb69929951c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "125ed0b3-14ad-5af8-8370-ca6f83d93c35" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455469}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase"} :step/id #uuid "a946c7fa-5aee-54d2-b223-0a3b560b3401" :step/math-academy-id 615693 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152296 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151900 :question/id #uuid "95c3dedd-accd-57dc-91ee-4ee8fdb2f3a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f90ea994-79f2-5409-bf96-306803b4ef01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542120}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying a Percentage Increase: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "fd57418f-a99a-5c17-9192-9191eca0084b" :step/math-academy-id 615696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153340 :tutorial/content "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more. \n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90?$\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90,$ we multiply $90$ by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0514684-2b6b-52ce-9ccb-6d28ed8d4850" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15090 :tutorial/title "Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "a3bd6e9e-9d93-51a6-9a20-5e9292cf93f4" :step/math-academy-id 615700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152297 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151901 :question/id #uuid "4af9bc31-4980-5c3c-9025-45e85ac09d8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21517"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88d0f4ad-a3f9-5275-b5d5-a9973bf63ebf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050618}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536824}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "d9b4f520-6201-594a-907d-e7a899c8d57c" :step/math-academy-id 615701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151913 :question/id #uuid "92d9c3ca-6e59-5c73-b640-114dfb7d13cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-22093"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb0103d-d686-575a-8ef8-81640227f4dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455511}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Word Problems Involving Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "10eca415-b601-5bc8-80c2-c2db5a9c451e" :step/math-academy-id 615704}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152315}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523} :activity/id #uuid "332f39d2-fe5b-5fe5-bfa7-8da0dfbe5393" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048502 :topic/id #uuid "a4f450cf-8fc5-50db-946a-e2e69ec67915" :topic/math-academy-id 2560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a39d413-aa71-523a-b6b3-dae4d712baf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485945} :answer-field/id #uuid "290d7c72-0a37-54c1-928d-b289c4e12639" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf1e4b17-b634-5d17-aa2d-098e82d8022f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327654" :question/problem "A factory produced $900$ boxes of cereal last year. This year, production is $300%$ greater than last year. How many boxes of cereal did the factory produce this year?\n\nThe factory produced\n{{field-1}}\nboxes of cereal this year." :question/worked-solution "To find this year's cereal production, we apply a $300%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $300%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $4:$\n\n$$\n900\\times 4=3,600\n$$\n\nSo, the factory produced $3600$ boxes of cereal this year."} :step/id #uuid "00e71b2a-765d-5d7f-a25b-d91d73442118"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a00a9b-f175-5563-a7e5-e9656e27a637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485935} {:answer/id #uuid "758ee6d2-143b-5d0c-85c7-1b00d67826f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485969} {:answer/id #uuid "974abbd8-6ac1-5b5c-809b-ef8a1790dbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} {:answer/id #uuid "aca33ef9-b028-5c98-a62b-f95962819c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486076} {:answer/id #uuid "be2c74de-5ea2-597a-8fc7-7510c00cdf16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486038} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3f1f35-966b-596c-862d-3ab162b9470c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa7ba5f1-091b-5461-b064-b9a572c0acc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318357" :question/problem "What number is $155%$ greater than $40?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $155%$ *greater than* $40,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+155%=255%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n255%=\\frac{255}{100}=2.55\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $155%$ greater than $40,$ we multiply $40$ by $2.55:$\n\n$$\n2.55\\cdot 40=102\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $155%$ greater than $40$ is $102.$"} :step/id #uuid "aa852896-f641-5bf3-81c3-1ddb33a1c5d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e18203-4dbf-5257-84e1-5d825b64f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,000,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485764} {:answer/id #uuid "01c47803-fa37-5ad4-a822-f030d4115e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,050,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485767} {:answer/id #uuid "22fa57f8-4bd0-583b-b691-d351b66f3152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,075,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} {:answer/id #uuid "26239f53-9cc0-56a7-acb2-66f332c11f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,100,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485828} {:answer/id #uuid "623d64e1-d70f-5584-bac1-b87f99bf2302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5,\\,025,\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485824} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4f452c-1a22-5679-9e96-6dcc369d76d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceab7d75-8909-5909-a769-ff36bb9c7667" :question/math-academy-id "q-326524" :question/problem "A country welcomed $3,\\,500,\\,000$ tourists in $2018.$  Travel authorities predict that this number will increase by $45%$ by the year $2028.$ Which of the following is closest to the projected number of tourists in $2028?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the projected number of tourists in $2028,$ we apply a $45%$ increase to the $2018$ figure.\n\nIncreasing by $45%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+45%=145%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the new value by multiplying the original amount by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n3,\\,500,\\,000\\times 1.45=5,\\,075,\\,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projected number of tourists in $2028$ is $5,\\,075,\\,000.$"} :step/id #uuid "ad0e7bc5-f3f5-5418-b7be-7515dd52c1f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486505 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2bf66f-071b-599c-870d-beb08d151c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$14.00$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485789} {:answer/id #uuid "81d65c1e-4fea-5d3d-b067-2a903bca796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.75$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485993} {:answer/id #uuid "9a524f5e-5406-5bdc-81e7-caf6bc6d50a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$12.95$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486041} {:answer/id #uuid "f89dfbd4-8787-51f2-b071-d16433f03c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486213} {:answer/id #uuid "fccdd420-a0f2-5e8c-abe0-1561c37428e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$13.14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486226} :answer-field/id #uuid "901b558e-ee47-587e-b837-3417a076e126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f6e2bde-069f-5dbd-8445-437abb3c38f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326517" :question/problem "A sandwich costs $$12$ before tax. If a $9.5%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" :question/worked-solution "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $9.5%$ increase to the original cost.\n\nIncreasing by $9.5%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+9.5%=109.5%=1.095\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.095:$\n\n$$\n12\\times 1.095=13.14.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price after tax is $$13.14.$"} :step/id #uuid "af48d2fc-c0ee-5116-9e15-35ee93f0388c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7cc659-73e4-532b-99a7-3c634766ec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ddf9b-1b91-530e-8514-164da8ccae4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485981} {:answer/id #uuid "b58bfefd-ae8c-59b0-b73a-de23da9e8c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486088} {:answer/id #uuid "c94777e7-dc40-5649-ab0e-9b864344dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486127} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed7cc-6a7f-517f-944e-d4397979f27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94424b9-97e6-5016-9825-443b370a2c58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62ed2686-5fd2-570f-9e2c-651f1d63d801" :question/math-academy-id "q-318127" :question/problem "What number is $125%$ greater than $64?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $125%$ *greater than* $64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+125%=225%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n225%=\\frac{225}{100}=2.25\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $125%$ greater than $64,$ we multiply $64$ by $2.25:$\n\n$$\n2.25\\cdot 64=144\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $125%$ greater than $64$ is $144.$"} :step/id #uuid "ff1f3d10-46ad-5cfc-b250-e2375bf213df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486503}}] :activity/title "Applying Percentage Increases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485754}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697} :activity/id #uuid "cade22aa-6331-58b6-892c-984d4c3bae76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548 :topic/id #uuid "a8f3106d-906d-52d1-b148-e532348d89d2" :topic/math-academy-id 79} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055997 :tutorial/content "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e82b56d-3e2d-582a-b73f-3c5c98830a58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d5ad5bf3-9168-540f-8492-ce7c9fc4b988" :step/math-academy-id 40308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :question/id #uuid "b8742776-1dc7-5816-afb6-9a72142ecb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3a71018-8f67-53cb-aa6c-7f17e0af9ef9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475967}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by the Number of Terms It Has"} :step/id #uuid "38cccf7d-917a-5a79-aa78-f1a2b54e1a10" :step/math-academy-id 40309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055998 :tutorial/content "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed2578f7-b1e4-5f83-b963-d66d7b97fa9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6661 :tutorial/title "Names of Polynomials Based on Their Degree"} :step/id #uuid "ac189d1f-bdee-52ff-86b2-d371b3d6e1c6" :step/math-academy-id 40312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :question/id #uuid "6270e77f-6ccb-5304-90da-2419fa309007" :question/math-academy-id "e-10651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048406}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533990}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"} :step/id #uuid "08e0cbe9-22f1-5b66-a44f-4af1547552f5" :step/math-academy-id 40313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :question/id #uuid "3400928d-9463-56f2-af47-e193a1e18a47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493921}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "227abef4-c6c1-5c73-9556-5ca01a686b40" :step/math-academy-id 40316}] :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054924}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493977} :activity/id #uuid "9d9c15c2-a381-503f-8c20-095ae4a1ed50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548 :topic/id #uuid "a8f3106d-906d-52d1-b148-e532348d89d2" :topic/math-academy-id 79} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1056a69e-6f45-5662-8064-9d31764878d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quadratic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493420} {:answer/id #uuid "683a7cea-5988-5760-91b7-9df2a330db1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quartic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493546} {:answer/id #uuid "71053b83-643d-5ef2-8a38-901d38fcd2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quartic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493557} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee88caf-fa42-587f-9b4e-37e2a9c5dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cubic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493617} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cubic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493686} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ac3cf1-ce85-58c6-8c68-c0b075726ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" :question/math-academy-id "q-51576" :question/problem "Classify the polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ according to its degree and the number of terms." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic trinomial."} :step/id #uuid "05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493537} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3ca694-1f66-5b36-b822-4d155549fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493585} {:answer/id #uuid "a66f3f1f-29ec-5be2-8bc5-722c8e836b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493654} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b1e4de-0805-5bfa-8e45-f45979420ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493753} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a93d32-18e7-5f8e-961d-886b53773bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493537} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a727332-6125-5bf9-8eee-55ebc8cbefb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152221" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The sum of $4{x}^{2}+1$ and $2{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The sum of $3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $5$ is a binomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a binomial and a monomial is **sometimes** a binomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+1)+(2{x}^{2})=6{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich is a binomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(3{x}^{2}-2x)+(5)=3{x}^{2}-2x+5,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a trinomial).\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of a binomial and a monomial *could be* a binomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n However, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *not always* a binomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n So, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *sometimes* binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6c22d6-e892-5446-9098-76a0b21515ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "linear binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493442} {:answer/id #uuid "32c65879-0672-5962-bea0-b0afdace1faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quadratic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493477} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quintic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493489} {:answer/id #uuid "44e3a524-e613-5db6-91fb-801225462723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cubic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493506} {:answer/id #uuid "8caae5ad-2091-5a94-9de8-16bbe05ea834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quartic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493489} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfb5c23-969b-59ed-8fd1-dd67557eb34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" :question/math-academy-id "q-111223" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $5,$ so it is a quintic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quintic binomial."} :step/id #uuid "39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493992}}] :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493384}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120934} :activity/id #uuid "18514bce-156b-56a7-b919-0aa8cd6b4e23" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770 :topic/id #uuid "bd442719-9650-5137-aa05-949d5ce4809c" :topic/math-academy-id 1819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121142 :tutorial/content "Finding the **cube root** of a number is the reverse of cubing a number. We express the cube root using the **radical symbol** $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}.$\n\nFor example, the \"cube root of $8$\" equals $\\color{blue}2$ since \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^3 = {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}2} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, we write $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nSome basic cube roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{0} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}} & = & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{1}^{3}} & = & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{2}^{3}} & = & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}} & = & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{4}^{3}} & = & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} & = & 5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}} & = & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA **perfect cube** is a number whose cube root is a whole number. So, the numbers \n\n$$\n0,\\qquad 1,\\qquad  8,\\qquad  27,\\qquad  64,\\qquad 125, \\qquad 1\\,000\n$$\n\n are all perfect cubes. You need to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "82bf149b-fb3b-506e-9c7b-3d6716081eaa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8377 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bc943f83-ca68-59a5-8d04-1f3be7f4da3e" :step/math-academy-id 554687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119524 :question/id #uuid "71372a52-1de2-5535-9216-6d90db0afc49" :question/math-academy-id "e-13374"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32268c05-9166-5798-81fc-ac3a99d966e0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475979}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Cubed Integer"} :step/id #uuid "41a11614-97fd-50d6-ae34-9ed01ba0df21" :step/math-academy-id 554688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119525 :question/id #uuid "60f95f30-9f10-5fe5-b5b6-f7b74e219a57" :question/math-academy-id "e-13375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e588edf8-345e-57e7-b3ee-9c3666da08e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534026}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Positive Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "1893b834-1078-578f-b788-398a16705469" :step/math-academy-id 554692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121143 :tutorial/content "Unlike the square root, it *is* possible to find the cube root of a negative number.\n\nFor example, $\\sqrt[3]{-8}={\\color{blue}{-2}}$ because\n\n$$\n-8 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})^3 = ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}})\\cdot ({\\color{blue}{-2}}).\n$$\n\nSome basic cube roots of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-1} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-1)^{3}} & = & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-8} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-27} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}} & = & -3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-64} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-4)^{3}} & = & -4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-125} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-5)^{3}} & = & -5 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-1\\,000} & = & \\sqrt[3]{(-10)^{3}} & = & -10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the numbers \n\n$$\n-1,\\qquad  -8,\\qquad  -27,\\qquad  -64,\\qquad -125, \\qquad -1\\,000\n$$\n\nare all perfect cubes." :tutorial/id #uuid "02c87b9c-9741-553f-a98b-da7832928f6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8378 :tutorial/title "The Cube Root of a Negative Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "8b332403-62f5-5fe0-ac76-52a66e2c10b5" :step/math-academy-id 554696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119526 :question/id #uuid "5c5c9efb-7bff-5177-a736-b21ce6410871" :question/math-academy-id "e-13376"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b9e5026-ab54-5f35-a8da-b647c0f68c1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533980}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Negative Perfect Cube"} :step/id #uuid "b172821f-393c-54f4-bec3-98560d9d0245" :step/math-academy-id 554697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121144 :tutorial/content "Let's calculate $(\\sqrt[3]{64})^3$ by performing each operation one step at a time:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}64}\\right)^3 = \\left(\\sqrt[3]{{\\color{red}4}^3}\\right)^3 = {\\color{red}4}^3 = {\\color{blue}64}\n$$\n\nNotice that cubing the cube root gives the number under the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\sqrt[3]{64} \\right)^3 = 64\n$$\n\nThis result holds in general. If $\\color{blue}\\square$ is any number, then\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\color{blue}\\square}\\right)^3 = {\\color{blue}\\square}.\n$$\n\nThe cube and the cube root cancel each other out, and we're left with the number that we started with.\n\n![](images/78/784f43f7253e1ed6fa394462a172e22571af06a5318b424ba2eab2157333c6fe.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "900df988-f097-53fe-8a55-39641f798d4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8379 :tutorial/title "Cubing a Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "b06bda82-f4c5-5f02-bffa-cfb125aada53" :step/math-academy-id 554701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119527 :question/id #uuid "fc571b6d-1dfe-5344-99c0-d6d2f5d1f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c331c742-c620-5e3c-ae77-37de9ded75cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534043}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube of the Cube Root of a Positive or Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "2768c295-f0d4-5823-a374-5050e8220981" :step/math-academy-id 554702 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121145 :tutorial/content "We can think of the cube root geometrically. For instance, let's represent the number $8$ by a collection of eight items:\n\n![](images/36/3677ea5b5f875e12fdcc6376f6b29b43a663495d20a3fbf69b4937cc61661d79.png)\n\nNow, we can pile them up in the form of a cube where each side is equal to $\\sqrt[3]{8}={\\color{blue}2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "859f5c79-ff9d-5b6d-91b3-305863a271c1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8380 :tutorial/title "A Geometric Approach to the Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "f9786bea-a98c-554e-ac43-50ebe1956098" :step/math-academy-id 554705}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493995} :activity/id #uuid "9b8dddd1-da8a-515b-b440-7da08015b94d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048770 :topic/id #uuid "bd442719-9650-5137-aa05-949d5ce4809c" :topic/math-academy-id 1819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11db075b-d65e-51e5-8dca-f8e9ab1adb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493421} {:answer/id #uuid "5c730b9a-1755-532b-b8d1-24d3aece98b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493533} {:answer/id #uuid "671ff086-c9db-58f6-af7a-4edb329a1ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493544} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0b316d-8f3a-5a45-a97f-136faefce7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493609} {:answer/id #uuid "f015c16e-7bc0-5eb8-9649-05d8fe751886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493609} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a4987cd-b000-5d3d-b94d-9c8d5cd48460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e1e6dba-6746-5b7e-9ed7-77e1ee7e8c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-71494" :question/problem "$(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "The cube and the cube root cancel each other out. So, we have\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{1,000})^{3}=1,000.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b1fdc86-b563-5301-a67d-cba49bff52be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbd15cf-50e0-5f6c-a023-66b7777a5ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493467} :answer-field/id #uuid "77156935-a293-5f77-b17e-01ad81fabeda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "03b02208-a47d-5484-9a1d-0e9d1a5e06da" :question/math-academy-id "q-186156" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $125$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{125}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{125}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{5}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "734e7353-a71f-5a59-9552-8bb9d82e349e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa5916f5-b95b-5530-9d9f-19d0168e2209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493658} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90a68c7-dc57-5362-b958-0dc11f3f0c40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aafcb24f-fde2-52b2-b44a-c7d7fddbc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-186155" :question/problem "Evaluate the following:\n\n$\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the numbers $27$ and $8$ are both perfect cubes. \n\nSo, to determine the value of $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}},$ we express $\\frac{27}{8}$ as a perfect cube:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{27}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4a52c70-3918-5a00-b200-12e071807393" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494013}}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493383}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb